        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Libertarianism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1943 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke">John Locke</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/two-treatises-of-government">Two Treatises of Government</a> (1689) set out liberal political theory; William Godwin, who took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a> to an anarchic conclusion; Joseph Déjacque, the first to call himself a libertarian; H. L. Mencken and Albert Jay Nock, the first prominent Americans to use the word as a synonym for liberal; Murray Rothbard, who popularised the term in its American sense; Robert Nozick, who argued for a minimal state; Hillel Steiner and Peter Vallentyne among left-libertarians</span></div>
