    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Libertarianism holds negative liberty and personal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> as primary values, and many libertarians connect freedom with the non-aggression principle: each person may live as they choose so long as they do not initiate force or fraud against others.</li><li>The word was first used for a new set of political positions by Joseph Déjacque in 1857, when libertarianism was a form of anarchist or left-wing politics; in the mid-20th century American proponents of free markets took it up, and it came to be widely associated with the right.</li><li>Scholars sort libertarians along left–right or socialist–capitalist lines by their views of property and capital: right-libertarians defend private property in land and the means of production, left-libertarians hold natural resources to belong to everyone or to be owned collectively.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Libertarian was already in print in 1796, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Packet reported 450 “French Libertarians” marching out of the prison at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a>, and there it meant a supporter of republicanism.</p></aside>
    </section>

